  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1233 AM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nFriday...As the main upper low continues pushing to the north \nthrough the day, another piece of energy separates and drops south \nacross southern CA, becoming a secondary closed low over southern CA \nand AZ. As this is occurring, strong southwest support for rainfall \nacross western CO drops off so rainfall rates decrease dramatically, \nand the Flood Watch will be allowed to expire at 00z Sat. Colder air \npushes in, dropping snow levels through the night. The northern low \ncontinues east as an open trough, forcing a cold front south across \neastern WY. Rain totals of an additional .40 of an inch for the \neastern San Juans, and up to a tenth of an inch elsewhere, are \nforecast, then showers and isolated thunderstorms push along the \nsouthern border and up across the far southeast corner through the \nlate afternoon and evening. This will be the last really warm day \nfor the eastern plains as temps once again climb into the 70s to \naround 80F, while the high valleys climb into the 60s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...A cold front pushes south into eastern CO \nSaturday morning, while the southern closed low moves up across AZ \nand reaches the Four Corners by Sat night. This will produce strong \nsouth to southwest flow, this time situated across the eastern half \nof the state. Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected on \nSaturday, with excessive rainfall amounts possible for the eastern \nplains, and new accumulating snow for the mts as snow levels drop to \naround 9500 ft late that night. On Sunday the upper low starts to \nopen up and track east into CO, continuing to bring rain and storm \nchances to the plains, while snow chances start to diminish. Plan on \nmuch cooler temps both days, with highs in the 50s for the high \nvalleys, and 50s to lower 60s for the plains.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...Upper low weekend, opens and tracks east \nacross CO as an open wave on Monday, with just some lingering \nisolated to low end scattered showers across the CWA. Temporary \nbroad ridging aloft then develops across the region for Tuesday and \nWednesday. Monday looks to be another very cool day, then a gradual \nwarming trend into midweek with temps climbing back up to seasonal \nnormals. Moore",
